  "requirement": "Every JSON file directly under `evidence/` MUST correspond to a declared requirement or named example. Habeas MUST report evidence JSON files that do not, and evidence JSON files in any other location under `evidence/`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Implementation.** The `{impl}` marker is at `src/evidence.rs:161`, in `inspect_repository_with_progress`.\n- `files_with_extension` (marked `{impl}` for `requirements.ci.coverage`) walks `evidence/` recursively and collects every `*.json` file. It does not follow symlinks.\n- For each file, the loop derives a name only when the path is directly under `evidence/`, meaning it has a single path component. It strips `.json` and looks the name up in the declared definitions, excluding glossary terms.\n- If there is no match, the file is reported as `evidence/...: no matching requirement or example`. That covers an unmatched top-level file and every nested file. Nested files always get `name = None`, so they are always reported.\n- A file that matches a declared requirement or example is added to `present`, and `validate_evidence_file` then checks its contents. The `present` set is used by the separate coverage check, which is marked for `requirements.ci.coverage`.\n- I found no behaviour in the marked code that the requirement does not specify. The extra schema validation, the coverage check and the progress reporting carry their own `{impl}` markers.\n\n**Tests.**\n- `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs::nested_evidence_files_are_reported` first validates a clean fixture. It then copies a valid record to `evidence/nested/rules.ready.json` and `evidence/rust/src/lib.rs.json`. It asserts that both are reported with the exact `no matching requirement or example` message.\n- `src/evidence.rs::collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` writes a valid but undeclared top-level `evidence/ghost.json`. It asserts that `no matching requirement` is reported.\n- `fixture` in `tests/evidence_boundaries.rs` supports these tests and is marked `{test}`.\n\n**Gaps.** The only untested corner is a top-level file named after a glossary term. The code handles it, because terms are filtered out of the match, so I do not count it as a gap.",
